# Clean code

- Understand the intended behavior, callers, and contracts before changing code.
- Naming and design judgment live here. Syntax and module conventions live in ts-js-style.mdc. Apply an overlapping point once.
- Apply a rule only to a concrete problem. Do not invent findings or edit code to satisfy a checklist.
- Give each function one coherent purpose. Do not split code only to make it shorter.
- Remove duplication only when both copies express the same rule. Do not merge similar syntax that encodes different policies.
- Add an abstraction only when it names a real concept callers use.
- Name the meaning, role, and units. Use one term for one concept and a different term for a different concept.
- Avoid a name that implies the wrong behavior or representation. Drop noise words and type encodings that add no distinction.
- Use nouns for things, verbs for actions, and questions for booleans. Use domain words for domain concepts and ordinary technical terms for mechanisms.
- Keep a short name only in a tiny local scope. Make a widely used name something a reader can say and search for.
- When renaming, update the references. Treat a public or stored name as a compatibility decision.
- Keep the steps in a function at one level of detail. Extract a helper only when its name adds a concept the caller did not already state.
- Make inputs, effects, and required order visible. Do not hide a dependency in global state just to remove a parameter.
- Split a flag that selects different operations when separate names make the call clearer. An existing boolean is not automatically a defect.
- Do not let a query change state as a surprise. Handle failure deliberately and keep the information a caller needs.
- Treat length, parameter count, and return count as signals, not quotas.
- Do not import Java class, factory, exception, or single-exit patterns into this JavaScript.
